Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes. wrote:
> 
> In article <32498A9E.5A3@cadvision.com>, Larry Craton <dashl@cadvision.com> writes:
> >
> >Investigating further it seems that only the first logical block
> >restored from the tape is correct (on the 8400) and that data
> >past that point is scrambled (verified by using dd and cmp).
> >If I use dd on the 8400 I can make the first N bytes restore
> >correctly by using bs=N (for N up to 200MB) but data from the
> >2nd block on is scrambled.
> 
> First, what you are using for the backup?  Dump/restore are not
> interchange program and you can't expect a dump made one operating
> system to be read on a different one.  For interchange you should
> use tar or cpio.
> 
> Assuming you are, what is block size used on the SCO system?  Both
> tar and cpio support options to use a block other than the default,
> which could be different than what is used on the SCO system.
> 
> >Larry
> 
> --
> Alan Rollow                             alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com

Ah..  The problem turned out to be a SCSI tape issue on the SCO
side.  A SCO patch for variable tape blocking was required in order
for cpio to work correctly.  (BTW: kudos to DEC software support
for pointing me in the right direction for this problem.)

Thanks, -Larry



