In article <l4afudkjuz.fsf@congo.llnl.gov>, Kevin Oberman            <oberman@es.net> writes:
|> Tina Stewart <stewart@wrc.noaa.gov> writes:
|> > Is there a limit to the number of IP addresses one can assign a NIC?
|> > The system is a DEC Alpha/400 with 128 MB RAM, and both an Ethernet 
|> > card and an FDDI card.  Has anyone set up a dozen or so Virtual Hosts on
|> > a similar system?
|> 
|> Last I saw, it was 256 aliases, but that was 3.2 and may have changed
|> in 4.0.

As far as I know, there was never a firm upper limit on the number
aliases, but with too many aliases installed, performance might
suffer on our older systems.

In V4.0, Digital UNIX uses a hash table to store aliases (actually,
a hash table pointing to a set of lists), whose size is configurable
(see the "inifaddr_hsize" variable in the "inet" subsystem).  I'm
not sure what the officially supported maximum number is now, but
it's certainly above 256 and probably a lot larger.

-Jeff



