Hello,
I have to get an ASE Environment working. Take a look at the details, if
you have any experience in this field!

Details:
I have two AlphaServer 2100 running Digital Unix V3.2d and a SW800
storage assembly. The disks within are controlled via HSZ40 Raid5
controllers with firmware V2.7Z-0.
We bought ASE V1.3 to install a failover concept, that is when the first
server can't do anything more the services defined (which can be disk-,
NFS- or user defined services) fail over to the second server living up
to this moment in pure stand by. Clients refer just to a virtual service
IP address and should just get a disconnect error.

We got the servers running an ASE installation all right with one single
network attachment.
The aim is to define a backup network so that the servers do not perform
a failover when just one of the two network paths break.

As far as I understood the concept and the manuals the two network paths
(that is the servers IP addresses on the two installed network
interfaces on each server) have to be different domains (e.g. 202.2.41.
and 202.2.42.).
The clients installed in the isle network have just one network
interface and are all in the same domain (e.g. 202.2.41.). As far as I
am familiar with TCP/IP there is no other way to let a 202.2.41.
interface communicate with a 202.2.42. interface (or any other domain)
than via a routing mechanism (which might be a SW or a dedicated router
HW).
Digital Consulting try to convince us that there is no need for routing,
but that we have just to define the IP addresses of the installed
interfaces in the same domain (e.g. 202.2.41.1 and 202.2.41.2). My tests
showed me, that Digital Unix does not activate the second network
interface (ifconfig reports the second interface to be down), so this
seems not to be the solution.
As far as I saw, there is no way in informing ASE or introducing any
kind of procedure within ASE to switch network interfaces before
performing a complete server fail over.

Has anyone out there an ASE installation with backup networks?
Who is so kind to share experiences with me?

Thanks for you attention an patiens!
Regards,
   Igor

