Well, he might get that from the NetWorker mailing lists.  But
from the October LPCD release of the NetWorker server for Digital UNIX
all the Digital releases (NT on alpha, and Digital UNIX) will be
numbered according to the Legato base code they are based on... the 
confusion of having two different release numbers is not worth the
trouble.  

## All you have to do to make it talk to another NetWorker server (Legato's
## or any Legato OEM's version, UNIX or non-UNIX) is to make sure you have
## the appropriate client license at the server.

The whole point of NetWorker is to use it in a heterogeneous
environment.  All servers and clients should work together, although
new server features can't be used by old clients and features
in new clients can't always be handled by old servers (pools
is an example of that).  There is extreme case where an old unix
server (many platforms) does not know about the remote execution
daemon and the current NT client only uses that - so that won't
work.  Such a server wouldn't be able to handle multiple jukeboxes,
pools, cloning, etc...  

#I initially balked at this statement because I thought I remembered 
#reading that one could not have a Digital client of a non-Digital server,
#but after perusing the Addendum for BRX321 (version 3.2A) I see that the
#actual statement is:
#
#	"Digital UNIX client files cannot be restored on non-Digital server
#	or client systems."
#
#That isn't quite the restriction I remembered reading, but it's important
#information.

The real language would be somewhat difficult to word.  Some files
and file systems (ufs) can be recovered by other unix systems, but
AdvFS filesystems and anything large (dependent on 64 bits of the OS)
are not.  AdvFS libraries are not available on other operating systems
and I don't think there is another 64 bit OS with a NetWorker client
just yet.  So hopefully, the reasons are obvious why restoring to
a non Digital UNIX system (NT on alpha won't work) won't work.

