David Starks-Browning wrote:
> It seems rather absurd to me that dU would kill cron or inetd rather than
> a user application, preferably the one consuming too much memory.

I agree that the method in selecting a victim process can be improved,
however, the process with the most memory may *not* be the one to kill.
It can be difficult for the system to decide which process on the system
is the least important, it often depends on what the system is being
used for.

> Is there some way to avoid this behavior?

You can run in eager swap mode or increase the amount of swap space so
that the system has enough (this may take a bit of trial and error).

