In article <5265p0$ks4@peabody.colorado.edu>,
Blake Kritzberg <kritzber@ucsub.Colorado.EDU> wrote:
>Hi, all --
>
>I'm having a hard time setting up quotas on a DEC alpha
>running OSF/1.
>
>So far, I've:
>- added an entry to /etc/fstab (options: rq)
>
>/dev/rz3c       /usr/local      ufs rq 1 2
>
>- touched the quota.user and quota.group files with chmod 600 by root
>at the top level of the desired filesystem
>
>- turned on quotaon for that filesystem
>
>---
>...but when I quotacheck that filesystem:
>
># quotaon /usr/local
># quotacheck /usr/local
>/usr/local not found or not properly enabled for quotas in /etc/fstab
>
>I took a brief look at the files list in /usr/sys and
>OPTIONS quota appears there, so it does look as if quota
>enabling's been compiled into the kernel. (Is there a way
>to check for sure?)
>
>Am i missing some steps here? Please set me straight!

For one, you have the order of commands wrong (check before enable);
for two, you probably can't touch quota.user and quota.group, as it expects
they're either formatted or absent, but not empty; for three, "rq"
is obsolete - try this (from the fstab(4) man page):

/dev/rz3c       /usr/local      ufs rw,userquota 1 2

Try this:

(1) Turn quotas off (quotaoff -a)
(2) Remove quota.user and quota.group
(3) Fix /etc/fstab
(4) Build the quota files (/sbin/quotacheck -a)
(5) Turn quotas on (quotaon -a)
