Thanks to all the people who responded about the password synchronisation 
problems we are having between Unix/NT and Oracle.  The original question
is attached below.

I received eight responses in total:

3 were asking for any results to be forwarded to them
2 suggested a product called GUARDIAN from Datalynx software 
          (datalynx@netcom.com)
1 suggested a product from DEC called Digital Password Synchronisation Software
1 suggested using Warp Server
1 suggested using expect and tcl to write the job to do it.

I haven't taken these much further yet as I was waiting to get all responses.
I will most likely follow up with the DEC product and the Guardian software.

Thanks again for all responses.
Ian Leonard

-----------------------------------------------------
Original question:


We are having problems at the moment, which I am sure lots of other people 
have experienced.  We now have a network with:

Windows NT Server, Win 95 and NT clients.
Unix machines running NIS (Digital Unix and HPUX)
Client/Server applications, Oracle server.

How do we keep our passwords in sync ?

We would like to enforce password ageing.  Unfortunately, we can't do this 
with NIS and the mixture of machines we have.  We can do it with Windows NT.
Not sure about the Oracle side of things.

How can we either:

1 - Have a single username and password database without forking out BIG
money for security products - smallish money is OK.

2 - use a utility on a PC Client that will change all the user passwords
at once.  The user can select the "Change Password" icon, this will then
change the Windows NT Password, the Unix password and 'other passwords' as
required.  If possible, can this be used as the password changer instead of
the standard Msoft one that would appear when your password has expired,
or changing your password through the control panel.

I have been looking for this sort of thing on and off for a year or
two now with no success.

Does anyone have any ideas ?  E-Mail responses would be appreciated, and
I will summarise if there are any answers to be had.
Thanks
Ian Leonard
leonardi@planning.wa.gov.au





