In article <3242CABD.41C6@lanl.gov> William Clodius <wclodius@lanl.gov> writes:

   Glad to see you active here again.  You have been pretty specific about
   what you want to do for 0.6 in addition temporarilly "fixing" its
   current stability, do you have any plans for 0.7 etc. beyond fixing
   0.6's final stability? Have you ever investigated getting funding from a
   government agency, i.e., NSF, NASA, DoD, or DOE?

Re 0.7, I don't see that as a stabilizing version of 0.6.  0.6 will be
stabilized in a 0.6.x series, just as 0.5 was stabilized in 0.5.x (0.5.18,
probably 0.5.19, and then destabilized to fix some internal problems in 0.5.20,
hopefully restabilized in 0.5.21 or .22, at which point that line probably
will end).

g77 is still in beta, and will be until it reaches 1.0.  There are a variety of
version-numbering schemes people use, but here is the one I'm using for now,
and would like to see g77 continue to use years from now:

  1.  GNU version numbering scheme, three fields only (major.minor.patch).

  2.  A new .patch version is not to be assumed to fix all the bugs in the
      previous one.  (Note that the current gcc maintainer believes gcc users
      expect this, hence the version after 2.7.2 was called 2.7.2.1, not 2.7.3.
      With g77, I'd call it 2.7.3.)

  3.  Before first product release, product is in "beta", and all version numbers
      are 0.minor.patch.

      a.  minor releases are really fairly major, and patches can be as well.
          The idea is that a new minor release signals some pretty fundamental
          changes in internals, compatibility, and so on.

      b.  patch releases might or might not be just bug-fixing.  Users, who are,
          by definition, beta testers, are expected to read the release notes and
          figure out for themselves whether they want to upgrade.  Usually, they
          should, but g77-0.5.20 might be one where some don't, or at least want to
          have 0.5.19 around for backup (this is my current plan).

  4.  First product release is 1.0.0.

      a.  It should be identical to some 0.minor.patch version, except for
          cosmetic and other non-functional changes, and hence should be very
          stable, since (presumably) that 0.minor.patch version turned out to be
          stable.

      b.  minor releases should not have any effect on documented product behavior
          in terms of compatibility with how the previous version was used, and
          should generally avoid substantial (more than low-risk) internal changes.

      c.  patch releases should only fix bugs, or add support for features, ports,
          etc. such that existing working use is unlikely to be affected.  (E.g.
          a g77 version 1.3.9 might add substantial new code that is invoked only
          when supported a hitherto-unsupported CPU or language feature.)

Linux used a scheme somewhat like the above.  I'm uncertain as to whether Linux's
post-1.0.0 approach of using _even_ minor numbers versus odd ones to distinguish
"experimenting with new features" from "making important, but not likely bug-inducing,
changes", but at least it might make sense to designate, for users a _particular_
major.minor "series" as "experimental", if the current state of the product requires
it.  I'm all for things like having web pages that have summaries of what things
were changed in various versions, of course.

As far as funding, I believe that acquiring funding takes significant chunks of time,
which I'd rather use to improve g77 for now, since I'd like to spend only another
several months working on g77 under the current scheme (i.e. as a volunteer, with
no supporting organization).  I have some ideas about finding, or perhaps creating,
an organization that would support free Fortran-related (or, more properly,
scientific/numeric products and services), and believe that the search for funding
would be undertaken by people in that organization who are better at it than I
(though I'd be happy to help out).  Whether I'd want to continue doing volunteer
work on g77, on other free scientific software, on free stuff in general, or get
paid for any of these, I can't say at this point.

Right now, I just want to get 0.5.x up to a functional level that will "hold off"
the masses until I complete 0.6, get both lines to a stable point (which is basically
easy for 0.5.x now, given how stable 0.5.18 has turned out to be), and start looking
into the whole issue of non-profit organizations for this field and other areas
in which I have quite an interest.

I still have less interest in earning gobs of money for myself than in solving
problems in, and introducing/implementing new ideas to, the information culture
in general.  And, though I don't use Fortran myself, it's hard to think of any
nifty idea I have, or any interesting problem I want to solve, that wouldn't be
best validated in some incarnation that caters to the Fortran audience, because
I have yet to see any persuasive evidence that there's any single, large group
of people that care more about information efficiency than the Fortran audience
(where they call it "optimization of generated code", of course ;-).
