In article <323FAEF4.5F24@grci.com>, Bob Blank  <bblank@grci.com> wrote:
>For some strange reason, my users .cshrc file is 
>excuting before their .login.

If you take a look at the csh(1) man page, you'll find this pretty close
to the top:

  When you run csh, it begins by executing commands from the file .cshrc in
  your home directory, if it exists.  If the shell is invoked with a name
  that starts with -, as when started by login, the shell runs as a login
  shell.  If csh runs as a login shell, it executes commands from the
  system-wide login file /etc/csh.login, if that file exists, and then com-
  mands from your $home/.cshrc file and your $home/.login file, in that
  order.
 
