references: <51r6mf$ld7@fstgal00.tu-graz.ac.at>
organization: Graz University of Technology, Austria
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Hi again,

I've solved it. The problem was the automounter. When copying the system
it was active. So the links from /tmp_mnt/.... to the automounted
directories were copied to the new target disk. After booting from
the new target disk, the automounted directories already had links
to /tmp_mnt/... set which apparently pretty much confused the automounter
and caused accesses to automounted directories to get stuck.

Now I've just re-booted with NFS mounts (including the automounter)
disabled, removed everything in /tmp_mnt and cleaned the automount
points to be empty directories rather than links into /tmp_mnt and
rebooted with NFS mounts enabled again. Now it works.

I should have copied the disks in single user mode.

Tom

