In article <3241C1B8.6BAF@t-rex.materials.unsw.edu.au> david pinson <dpinson@t-rex.materials.unsw.edu.au> writes:

   What needs to be done to get g77 to compile for a DEC Alpha?

There are some patches "in development" that make it work pretty well.

   I'm aware of the problems associated with the 64 bit chip/os, 
   but as I understand other people have compiled it, I'd like to
   know how.

I suspect they've used version 0.5.17, which didn't "check itself" and
thus crash when it detected inconsistencies.  But, support for COMPLEX,
and things like combining DATA with EQUIVALENCE of INTEGER and REAL types,
would either crash the compiler or produce incorrect results.

Version 0.5.18 catches some of the basic inconsistencies at startup, crashing
right away with the assertion you're seeing.

The patches in development fix all this, though there are probably still
bugs in the gcc back end that make the patched g77 less than a fully robust
compiler.  (The g77 front end has gotten pretty stable.  I plan on fixing this
for 0.6, by rewriting a major portion of it.  ;-)

I'm finally getting back to working on g77, so I expect to start catching up
on stuff like this over the next couple of months, then start working on 0.6.
