In article <danpop.842645393@news.cern.ch> Dan.Pop@cern.ch "Dan Pop" writes:

>In <842570087snz@genesis.demon.co.uk> fred@genesis.demon.co.uk (Lawrence Kirby)
> writes:
>
>>In article <DxKEKD.B04@cogsci.ed.ac.uk>
>>           richard@cogsci.ed.ac.uk "Richard Tobin" writes:
>>
>>>In article <danpop.842229249@news.cern.ch> Dan.Pop@cern.ch (Dan Pop) writes:
>>>
>>>>Within the frame of ANSI C, printing a time_t value is pointless,
>>>>because the way the time is encoded is unspecified.
>>>
>>>You might want to read it in again later.
>>
>>In that case just print it as:
>>
>>    printf("%Lf", (long double)t);
>
>But you may lose bits of precision if the bits of the time_t value don't
>fit in the mantissa of a long double.

OTOH double may well be considered enough since that is the limit of
the precision of difftime(). OK, there's no perfect solution however
double/long double will work on any reasonable implementation and even
where it isn't exact it is only likely to be inexact to a vary small
fraction of a second. There's a problem even when time_t is known to
be integral since there's no guarantee that you can uniquely map
all the values of long to unsigned long (or vice versa). So for the
ultimate belts-and-braces solution one you'd determined it was integral
you'd also need to test whether time_t is a signed or unsigned integral
type.

I'm still happy with long double.

