Hi:

I have an AlphaStation 400 4/233 running Digital Unix 3.2.  I periodically
check the state of the processes by doing a ps aux, and invariably find processes 
which are no longer valid; that is, somebody abandoned them for some time.  This 
happens, for instance, if a user gets 'hung' in an application, and ^z's out of it, 
but the process still is drawing on the machines resources.

Is there a way to target these 'hung' processes, so that I can implement a script to 
patrol the system, killing these types of processes?

Before I recently upgraded to 3.2(was at 3.0b), I often was able to identify these 
guys as 'T' in the 'state' field of a ps output; but now they seem to be 'I''s or 
'S's, so how do I tell them apart from the legit processes.

Thanking you in advance,


John A.


