Date: 1996/09/16
Message-ID: <323D2EEF.6F59@zko.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 181002475
references: <3236EC7D.15FB@dna406.dna.mci.com> <87d8zrf3cv.fsf@serpentine.com>
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.programming.threads,comp.unix.osf.osf1
x-mailer: Mozilla 3.0Gold (X11; I; OSF1 V4.0 alpha)


Bryan O'Sullivan wrote:
> 
> David Hinz wrote:
> d> On Digital UNIX 3.2c can asynchronous I/O and threads be used
> d> together?
> 
> I would expect so, but I can't imagine why anyone would want to do
> that.  Part of the motivation behind providing threads under Unix is
> that it allows you not to use cruft like aio.
> 
>         <b

Actually, Bryan, I can think of one reason to use async I/O with
threads. Just as threads are cheaper than processes, async I/O is
cheaper (in theory) than threads. That is, each async I/O context
includes less information, and can be manipulated more efficiently.

On the other hand, this is relevant only when you're doing a LOT of
concurrent I/Os, and you have real reason to expect them to be able to
proceed in parallel, and those parallel async I/Os can be handled in
series by a substantially smaller number of threads. (Some high
performance database engines, for example, use async I/O to help spread
disk accesses across striped spindles -- it takes a lot of smarts to do
this well, though.)

Anyway, the CAN be used together, and if you're having trouble, Dave,
you'll need to give more specific information for anyone to help you.

