>in C and C++ on Digital UNIX, as it does currently with the REAL*16
>type in FORTRAN.
>
>The current C and C++ compilers for Digital UNIX implement the ANSI C
>type "long double" as a synonym for type "double", i.e., a 64-bit
>type.  Because of this, we face some decisions around binary
>compatibility, and are looking for input.

Have you considered using the currently (bizarre) mechanism like
"long long" for a 64 bit integer, so you'd have a "double double"
(how's that for a suggestion).

I _had_ suggested (over a year ago) in comp.lang.c that we use more
interesting adjectives for these excessive sizes.  I _had_ suggested

		very long			- 64 bit
		really long			- 128 bit
		extremely long			- 256 bit
		incredibly long			- 512 bit
	and:	ludicrously long		- 1024 bit

This makes extending the "double" more interesting, to wit:

		double				- 64 bit
		long double			- 64 bit
		really long double		- 128 bit
		extremely long double		- 256 bit
		incredibly long double		- 512 bit
	and:	ludicrously long double		- 1024 bit
	
_That_ scheme would last a while, wouldn't it?

(Maybe I should've cross-posted this to rec.humor.funny...)

Seriously, I'm not sure I can understand the requirement for so much
precision since we're really most interested in enhancing the "range"
(magnitude) ove the actual precision.

If I recall aright back in my GeoPhysical processing days, IBM's 32bit
floating point number just barely fell short of being useful for
seismic trace analysis while the 36bit FP of the 1100 was adequate
and the 60bit FP of the Cray-1 was way too much.  (especially when
doing 3D FFTs).

_I'd_ be curious to know what kind of application requires this extreme
level of precision...
...but that's my curious streak.

