NIS. What we've done is nfs mount the authentication file to a second
system. (I wouldn't have to do this if DEC's NIS didn't choke when you
have a lot of logins in a short period of time).  This also involved
some really ugly hacking.  I had to rewrite the passwd, chfn and chsh
command, and move the /etc/passwd and /etc/group files to a shared
location.

Now the second system, the one that mounts the files with nfs, also
showed the same symptoms as the server.  When I rebooted the server,
the client immediately became a happy camper.  Therefore, the problem
would appear to be with something that was shared, and relevent during
login.  This would include, of course, the authentication files, but
also all the files run during login from each user's account since
these are all shared also.

