Richard Tobin wrote:
> In article <danpop.842229249@news.cern.ch> Dan.Pop@cern.ch (Dan Pop)
> writes:
> > it [time_t] can be any of the 9 integral types or 3 floating point
> > types.
> 
> There might be some trick for portably determining whether it's an
> integral or floating type.  How about
> 
>    (time_t)0.3 == 0
> 
> Am I right in thinking this expression is always legal and must be
> true iff time_t is an integral type?

Why would you ever want to do that?  You might thereby figure out
whether time_t is an integral or floating point type, but you still
can't do much directly with time_t values.  That's why there are
standard library functions for handling them and converting them to and
from more useful formats.
     If you try to use tricks like these, one of three things is likely
to happen:

          1. Your code is perfectly portable and equally unreadable.
          2. Your code should be portable but some compiler and library
                 writers with less legalistic minds than yours have
                 interpreted the standard in a sloppy way so your
                 code isn't really portable.
          3. You have overinterpreted the standard and your code only
                works as intended on a small number of machines.

This kind of fun you don't need.

