In article <50jmf2$1ul@horatius.ccn.net>, rp001ns@netsurf.de (rp001ns) writes:

There are two errors in this, 

>Backups should be made in
>single user mode, because unix will corrupt open files during 
>backup.

There are some problems that can happen when backing up a live
file system.  But the chance of them happening is a long way
from "unix will corrupt".  If you want the most reliable backup
from dump, you want to shutdown to single user.  Even then,
the root file system is still mounted.  If I open a file and
don't do anything to it, dump will hard pressed to corrupt
the backup of that file.

>... and to use advanced features of the storage works technologie.

LSM is independent of the StorageWorks subsystems.  The backplane
RAID subsystems use a bootable configuration utility and the HSZ40
family uses a tty console for its configuration.  Once upon a time
LSM was going to have a hook to allow running a host based configuration
utility, but it never got used.

