>Who said time_t is a scalar?

ANSI/ISO C.  But it doesn't say what kind of scalar:

    The types declared are size_t (described in $4.1.5); 

	     clock_t
    and 
	     time_t

    which are arithmetic types capable of representing times;
              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
So, it can be any of the 9 integral types or 3 floating point types.

>It might be something else, mightn't it?

Nope.  All the standard library functions returning a time_t value
return (time_t)-1 in case of failure.

Within the frame of ANSI C, printing a time_t value is pointless,
because the way the time is encoded is unspecified.

