Paul T. Wilson wrote:
> 
> My only comment to this Chuck is that in more than 18 years of programming
> I've yet to work with a "development environment" by which I assume you
> mean some capability that has some level of smarts and helps you create
> the program.  As a programmer I still need to know that int != long.
> Indeed, being familiar with the differences between platforms provides the
> necessary heads up if you haven't developed "portable" code.  Frankly,
> simply because portability was not a requirement much of the code I've
> written hasn't been "portable".  But, believe it or not, it's been ported.

I couldn't agree more.  In MY 18 years of software development I have
rarely been given a portability requirement and because of that when
the code finally was ported it was not done so as gracefully as it might
have been.  But then developing portable code is more expensive than
building to a standard platform so the savings over the lifetime of the
code on the old system may well have paid for the cost of porting it.
But then that may not be so if time to market is taken into account.

> 
> The discipline necessary to practice correct ("portable") programming is
> not, IMHO, instilled by a "development environment".  Not that I wouldn't
> like to program in one!

Again I agree.  Although you have far to grandiose an interpretation of
what I refer to as a development environment.  By development environment
I mean the environment in which you develop including the system (hardare
and OS) and the tools (a compiler and editor are tools).  What I do not
do is assume that the development environment is the same as the target
environment in which the app must run.  For a multi-platform system it
can't be and I would argue it doesn't have to be any of the targets.

I would want the development environment that provides me with the best
tools to do my job regardless of the target.  If I am developing a UI
for a VCR I don't want to develop on a VCR.

-- 
Chuck Dillon
