Dan Fineman wrote: 
> I'm trying to compile gnu tar 1.11.8 on a Dec Alpha running DUNIX 4.0
> (a.k.a. osf4.0).  Unfortunately, it just ain't happening.
> -----------------
> gcc -c -DHAVE_CONFIG_H -I.. -I../intl -I. -I../lib  -g -O tar.c
> tar.c: In function `name_match':
> tar.c:477: `FNM_LEADING_DIR' undeclared (first use this function)
> tar.c:477: (Each undeclared identifier is reported only once
> tar.c:477: for each function it appears in.)
> tar.c: In function `name_scan':
> tar.c:608: `FNM_LEADING_DIR' undeclared (first use this function)
> tar.c: In function `check_exclude':
> tar.c:828: `FNM_LEADING_DIR' undeclared (first use this function)
> *** Exit 1
> ----------------- 
> Anyways, I noted that on a linux system of mine,
> /usr/include/fnmatch.h has this line:
> ---------
> #define FNM_LEADING_DIR (1 << 3) /* Ignore `/...' after a match.  */
> ---------

Looks like you should change FNM_LEADING_DIR to FNM_NOESCAPE to
compile this code on Digital Unix.

From man page on fnmatch(3) 

FNM_NOESCAPE   	Unless FNM_NOESCAPE is set, preceding a character in 
	       	pattern with a \ (backslash character) causes fnmatch() 
	       	to match that character in string.  For example, \\ 
	     	matches a backslash in string.  If FNM_NOESCAPE is set, 
		\ (backslash) is interpreted as an ordinary character.

from /usr/include/fnmatch.h:
#define FNM_NOESCAPE    8       /* Don't treat backslash as an escape */

Concerning 1 << 3 = 8 and FNM_LEADING_DIR sounds to be functional
equivalent for FNM_NOESCAPE, I guess they are the same.

Hope this helps,
Gleb.



