In article <322DB1BE.6201@alf.dec.com>, Steve Hancock <hancock@alf.dec.com> writes:

[ Jonathan Chen appears to have written this part... ]
>> But if you're planning to use the file-sys as a news-server, use UFS.
>> --
>> ---------------------------------------------------------------------
>> Jonathan Chen <jonc@pinnacle.co.nz> | Opportunites are seldom labeled
>> ---------------------------------------------------------------------
>
>Not to be disagreeable, but AdvFS works fine for news servers if
>its properly configured and maintained. I have some tips that I
>routinely give customers when setting it up:
>
> [ And Steve goes to length on how AdvFS can successfully be
>   used with News. ]

Constant defragmentation, rebalancing, removing and adding volumes
to get the bit map table reallocated contiguously.  Seems like an
awful lot of work just to use a file system with News.  With UFS
it is fairly easy to ensure you'll probably never run out of files
as long as the files contain some data.  Give newfs the right
parameters up front and you're done.

Running News on AdvFS should not require constant maintenance just
to ensure News keeps running.  There should be more important things
to worry about.

>It the nature of the USENET application 
>that causes the file system problems, not the file system itself.

Wrong.  It is the nature of this particular application that reveals
a short coming in the design and implementation of the Advanced File
System.  News is not the only application that cycles through lots
of small files.  But, it is one of the most prevalent.  If the file
system could extend the BMT as necessary, independent of how many
times it had been extended in the past or of how contiguous the
free space was, this problem wouldn't exist.

AdvFS has it strong points and many of the worst problems have
been fixed in recent versions.  And it is nice to know that it
can be forced to work with News.  But, people shouldn't have to
perform this much maintenance just to store a few million small
files...

