In article <50l1nv$jev@clic3.qbc.clic.net>, tessierr@berclain.com (Richard Tessier) writes:

>I'm looking for a way to get the amount of memory that is available
>for a process. Under several other UNIX platforms (HPUX, SCO ...) we
>use the available space for the data (using sbrk and ulimit) but this
>concept doesn't work under OSF (since it can allocate non-contiguous
>memory blocks, the result of sbrk(0) can be over ulimit(UL_GETBREAK)
>even if there is no segmentation fault). Is there a way to do
>something similar(or better !) that would work on OSF 3.2 ?

You can use getrlimit(2) to get the various memory limits for
data, stack and text.  You can use table(2) to find out how
much page/swap space is available and reserved, which is
usually the first limit you run into when allocating lots.
You can infer the swap mode by looking for the link
/sbin/swapdefault, but that can be wrong (recently removed
and the system not rebooted yet).  The last I knew finding
out with certainty required reading from /dev/kmem.

There might be other system parameters that will limit virtual
memory beyond what the R-limits do, but the amount of page/swap
space, swap mode and those limits should be enough to get a good
clue.

>Richard Tessier                         Berclain Group Inc.
