wjjr@sapphire.alisa.org (John J. Rushford) writes:

> sizeof(int) == sizeof(long) is true on a lot of UNIX platforms not just Linux 
> or Solaris x86 and has been for a long time.  Just curious as to why you use 
> this criteria to judge which OS to use.

Take another look at the subject. For primary development, use the
platform that is most unforgiving when it comes to coding errors, and
port to more lenient platforms. The other way around it is a nightmare (believe
me--I've been there). Two of the most frequent coding errors in the
UNIX developer community (precisely because it is, as you say, common
to have int and long to be identical) is to confuse ints and longs,
especially when it comes to passing parameters, mixing pointers, and
*printf formats, and "tunneling" pointer values through int variables
and arguments. Try porting a 100.000+ lines of code application laced
with these bugs to Digital Unix *shudder*. If you code for dUNIX
first, you'll catch these buggers up-front. Plus, you learn to avoid them.



