Just for completness of your list, SCO OpenServer 5 has two kernel 
tunables to allow you adjust the behaviour.  

	'notice_null_refs' prints the PID, name, etc. of the process on
		the console.  Clearly, this is useful only if you're sitting
		at the console or are otherwise watching /var/adm/messages.

	'signal_null_refs' delivers a signal to the offenders.

Yes, it's smart enough to look at the object file format and know if 
a NULL deref really is valid.  For example, on an OMF (xenix) binary,
it's legal so it won't whine or signal.  For an ELF binary, it's never
legal, so it invokes the appropriate militant behaviour.


