In article <507igl$cm1@dogbert.cc.ndsu.NoDak.edu>,
mooney@dogbert.cc.ndsu.NoDak.edu (Tim Mooney) wrote:

> In article <3227327F.550C@fhk.smtp.usz.ch>,
> Christoph Nievergelt  <chn@fhk.smtp.usz.ch> wrote:
> > Assuming you have set the SCSI ID of the second disk to 1, do the
> > following:
> > 
> > cd /dev
> > ./MAKEDEV rz1
> > 
> > This should do the trick.
> 
> Assuming there's an entry in the kernel for the device, of course.
> 
> The cookbook approach is:
> 
> 1) cp /vmunix /vmunix.bak
> 2) cp /genvmunix /vmunix
> 3) shutdown -h +5 "Adding a disk... back in a few"
> 4) (once it has halted, at the console >>> prompt):
> 
>         boot -fl s
> 
> 5) (once you've booted to single user mode):
>     a) if you have LSM:
> 
>                 /sbin/lsmbstartup
> 
>         b) 
> 
>                 /sbin/bcheckrc
> 
> 6) (at this point you're in single user mode with filesystems mounted, now
>         build a new kernel):
> 
>         doconfig
> 
> 7) build the kernel as you normally would (see the admin guide if you're
> unfamiliar with this procedure).
> 
> 8) once the kernel is built,
> 
>         cp /sys/<your hostname in caps here>/vmunix /vmunix
> 
> 9) shutdown -r now
> 
> 
> 10) When the new kernel boots, you should see it mention the disk you've
> added to the system.
> 
> 11) at this point, the kernel knows about the drive.  Now just make some
> device files, as Chrisoph suggested above:
> 
>         cd /dev
>         ./MAKEDEV rz<whatever>
> 
> 
> The quicker way of doing this would have been to edit /sys/conf/<your hostname
> in caps>, and add an entry for the new disk (copy one of the existing disk
> device entries, and modify appropriately) and then rebuild the kernel like:
> 
>         doconfig -c
> 
> then just cp the kernel into place and reboot.  Steps 1-6 above essentially
> do this procedure for you.
> 
