Paolo Zuccon wrote:
< some stuff about crashing subshells >

Paolo, try this:
First, from your normal logged in shell, type

% set > my.set
% env > my.env

Then, put those two lines into the script you're trying
to run, as the first two lines, and try to run the script
the same way you have been.  Be sure to rename the output
files each time to something meaningful, or they'll be
overwritten.

Once you have all your environmental settings in files,
print them out and look at them.  Compare each one and
you should find some anomaly indicating the problem.

Failing that, I'd say try this:

Jump into a normal shell

% csh

Try to run your program.  If it runs, I've got no idea what
your problem is.  If it doesn't, it's your tcsh.
You've got something tcsh specific in the program.  Fix it.

One more idea to try as a work around.  Execute your program
as a background process and see if it runs.  This will at least
allow you to run the thing and get other work done.

