> Is HP-UX that bad platform ??
> 
> Probably there are more Unix systems with
> sizeof(int) == sizeof(long). But I don't think it's
> really important. 

If you are a very good developer who doesn't make the stupid kind of
mistakes we mere mortals make (and you don't have any code from mere
mortals to maintain or incorporate into your code), or if you
absolutely, positively know that you'll never port your code to a
platform where ints and longs are really distinct types, then it is
not important.

But my experience, people do make mistakes like

        time_t t;
        sprintf(buf, "%ld", t);

or

        long l;
        time(&l);

or
        int foo(long *l);
        int i;

        foo(&i);
        
or
        int foo(int i)
        {
                void *p=(void*)i;
        }

or

        ...

Believe me. It does happen (especially legacy code is full of this
kind of stuff). And if you have to weed these out during a port -- nightmare.



