>> me--I've been there). Two of the most frequent coding errors in the
>> UNIX developer community (precisely because it is, as you say, common
>> to have int and long to be identical) is to confuse ints and longs,
>> especially when it comes to passing parameters, mixing pointers, and
>> *printf formats, and "tunneling" pointer values through int variables
>> and arguments. Try porting a 100.000+ lines of code application laced
>> with these bugs to Digital Unix *shudder*. If you code for dUNIX
>> first, you'll catch these buggers up-front. Plus, you learn to avoid them.

A good compiler will warn you about these things. My makefiles tend to have
CC=gcc -ansi -pedantic -Wall -Wtraditional -Wpointer-arith -Wcast-qual \
	-Wcast-align -Wwrite-strings -Wconversion -Wstrict-prototypes \
	-Wmissing-prototypes -Waggregate-return
(and also -fshort-enums, but that's besides the point).
Parameter passing? Anything without a prototype will be flagged.
Prototype mismatches will be flagged. Cases where old K&R C
would implicitly change the passed type are flagged. 
Mixing pointers? Will be flagged too, albeit perhaps not quite as
strictly as with IBM's XL compilers for AIX.
*printf formats? Mismatches between int and long int are flagged.

Since gcc runs on many platforms, the only factor left is the quality
of each platform's /usr/include header files. I'm not sure which is most
unforgiving.



