Richard Abraham (abrahad@govonca3.gov.on.ca) wrote:
: I have been running httpd from a DEC 3000/300 alpha box.  I recently
: moved the server to an Alphaserver 2100 ( a much more powerful box).
: 
: The speed with which httpd serves my documents to other machines on the
: lan is a little quicker but the speed with which httpd serves my
: documents to machines coming in across a wan is 10 times slower from the
: Alphaserver 2100 than it was from my DEC 3000/300.  What gives.  I am
: running the same version on both machines.
: 
: Anyone with any insight into this please let me know.

I'm guessing that your IP over WAN users have a much longer RTT (round
trip time) for IP packets than your local users.

I'll also guess that the version of DUX on your 3000 is older than the
2100?

DEC is not the only Unix vender who in the latest OS release who _may_ have
screwed up the IP stack for slow links.  Sun with Solaris 2.5 has done
the same thing.

The issue is the ACk timers and retransmit timers have been re-tuned for
100baset and faster LANS at the expense of SLIP/PPP/slow IP paths.

Does any DEC TCP/IP stack tuner know how to re-tune the stack to be more
tolerant of slower paths??

Good luck.

Curt Smith
curts@compgen.com




